---
import Landing from "../layouts/Landing.astro";

const repo = "https://github.com/caioricciuti/ldf";

// Every figure below is copied from reports/2026-10-17-baseline
// (tokens-medium-2026-10-18.md and the "Size step" sections). o200k_base
// tokens, ratio to compact JSON, first 1 000 records of each dataset.
const tokenRows = [
  ["flat_users", "0.61", "0.65", "0.60"],
  ["nested_orders", "0.64", "1.08", "n/a"],
  ["sparse_events", "0.80", "1.22", "0.61, lossy"],
  ["wide_table", "0.43", "0.44", "0.43"],
  ["empty_values", "0.36", "1.27", "n/a"],
  ["deep_nesting", "0.80", "1.82", "n/a"],
];

// `ldf-mcp convert` output for the JSON below, byte for byte. Token counts
// are `ldf-bench count` (o200k_base) on the LDF and on the JSON in compact
// form; the JSON is shown wrapped, one record per line.
const example = `@records id:s,customer.name:s,items:t,total:f,status:s
ORD-1,Val Thompson,2,715.94,shipped
ORD-2,Sol Ritchie,1,2757.03,cancelled
ORD-3,Kim Abara,2,53.78,shipped
@records.items id:s,sku:s,qty:i,price:f
ORD-1,E5,3,28.78
ORD-1,C3,4,157.4
ORD-2,A1,1,2757.03
ORD-3,B2,2,12.5
ORD-3,E5,1,28.78`;

const json = `[{"id":"ORD-1","customer":{"name":"Val Thompson"},
  "items":[{"sku":"E5","qty":3,"price":28.78},
           {"sku":"C3","qty":4,"price":157.4}],
  "total":715.94,"status":"shipped"},
 {"id":"ORD-2","customer":{"name":"Sol Ritchie"},
  "items":[{"sku":"A1","qty":1,"price":2757.03}],
  "total":2757.03,"status":"cancelled"},
 {"id":"ORD-3","customer":{"name":"Kim Abara"},
  "items":[{"sku":"B2","qty":2,"price":12.5},
           {"sku":"E5","qty":1,"price":28.78}],
  "total":53.78,"status":"shipped"}]`;

const install = `cargo install ldf-mcp --locked
ldf-mcp convert data.json > data.ldf`;

// The server commands as any MCP client takes them (Claude Code, Codex CLI,
// OpenCode, ...); each client has its own way to register a command.
const mcp = `ldf-mcp proxy -- <any stdio MCP server> [its args]
ldf-mcp clickhouse --url http://127.0.0.1:8123
ldf-mcp files --root ./exports`;

const lib = `let text = ldf::encode(&records);   // &[ldf_data::Value]
let back = ldf::decode(&text)?;      // the same records`;

const hook = `ldf-mcp install claude-code --hooks --apply`;
---

<Landing
  title="LDF: tables a language model can read"
  description="Language Data Format: a lossless text encoding of JSON records for language models. Claude Opus 5.5 answers from it as well as from JSON at 57 to 77 percent of JSON's input tokens. Measured, not assumed."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container hero-grid">
      <div class="hero-copy" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Spec v0.2 · ldf-mcp 0.5</p>
        <h1 class="h1">Tables a language model can read.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          LDF turns JSON records into typed tables: one header, one line per record, child tables for
          nested arrays. Nothing is lost on the way in or out, and it works with any model that reads
          text. Measured on Claude, GPT and DeepSeek: on held-out data Claude Opus 5.5 answers from LDF
          as well as from JSON, at 57 to 77 percent of JSON's input tokens.
        </p>
        <div class="sample install">
          <div class="sample-bar"><span>Install, then convert</span><span class="dim">one binary, no runtime</span></div>
          <pre><code>{install}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="cta">
          <a class="btn btn-light" href="/docs/">Get started <span class="arrow">→</span></a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={repo}>Source on GitHub</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-sample" data-reveal>
        <div class="sample">
          <div class="sample-bar"><span>orders.ldf</span><span class="dim">143 tokens</span></div>
          <pre><code>{example}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="sample faded">
          <div class="sample-bar"><span>the same data as JSON</span><span class="dim">169 tokens compact</span></div>
          <pre><code>{json}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="sample-note">Three records, so the headers weigh a lot. Over hundreds of records LDF lands at 0.4 to 0.8 of JSON's tokens, see the numbers below.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <ul class="container facts" data-reveal>
      <li><strong>Lossless.</strong> Byte-exact round trip on ten of twelve benchmark datasets; the two sparse ones come back with keys reordered, nothing lost.</li>
      <li><strong>Chosen per model.</strong> LDF goes only to a model measured reading it as well as JSON on that shape of data. Every other model keeps getting JSON.</li>
      <li><strong>Measured.</strong> Five models, held-out data, sign tests. The misses are in the reports, not hidden.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="format">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">The format</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Eight rules. <span class="soft">That is the whole spec.</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">A document is a sequence of tables. Each table is a header line and rows. Everything else follows from the data.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rules" data-reveal>
        <div class="rule"><code>@records a:i,b:s</code><p>A header names the table and types each column: <code>i</code> integer, <code>f</code> float, <code>s</code> string, <code>b</code> boolean, <code>j</code> any JSON, <code>t</code> child table. <code>?</code> marks a column that may be null or absent.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>1,hello</code><p>A row is one record, cells separated by commas. Strings are bare unless they need quoting, then they are JSON strings.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>,</code> and <code>null</code><p>An empty cell means the key is absent. <code>null</code> means it is present and null. JSON's distinction survives.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>customer.name:s</code><p>Objects with a stable shape flatten into dotted columns. Irregular ones stay as a JSON cell.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>@records.items</code><p>Arrays of objects become a child table joined on the parent's first column. The parent keeps a count.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>items:[sku,qty]</code><p>New in v0.2, opt-in: an array of flat objects with one key order stays in its row as lists of values, keys once in the header. 17 percent fewer tokens on nested orders.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>~:j?</code><p>Keys present in fewer than half the rows travel in a small per-row object instead of a mostly empty column. Measured to matter for smaller models.</p></div>
        <div class="rule"><code>1.0</code> stays <code>1.0</code><p>Numbers keep their kind and shortest round-trip text. Integers above 2^53 do not get mangled.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="numbers">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Numbers</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Tokens, as a share of compact JSON.</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          o200k_base tokens on the first 1 000 records of the benchmark datasets, from the report's own
          tables. CSV ties LDF where CSV works at all; CSV cannot hold nested data and drops the
          difference between missing and null.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap" data-reveal>
        <table class="numbers">
          <thead><tr><th>dataset</th><th>LDF</th><th>TOON</th><th>CSV</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {tokenRows.map(([name, ldf, toon, csv]) => (
              <tr><td><code>{name}</code></td><td class="strong">{ldf}</td><td>{toon}</td><td>{csv}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="stats" data-reveal>
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-n">294 / 297</span><span class="stat-l">Claude Opus 5.5 on held-out data, LDF and JSON alike, with LDF at 57 to 77 percent of JSON's input tokens. <a href="/docs/results/stage-c/">Stage C</a></span></div>
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-n">53 / 53</span><span class="stat-l">GPT-6.1 Sol on LDF at 50 records, zero-shot: the prompt names the format and shows the data, nothing more.</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><span class="stat-n">0.56×</span><span class="stat-l">JSON tool output in real agent sessions (headless Claude Code, Haiku 5.5 and Opus 5.5), in provider-reported tokens, no answer lost. <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/reports/2026-10-20-hook/ab-opus`}>A/B runs</a></span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="note" data-reveal>Below the frontier tier no format is reliably better than another, and the reports say so. A model without enough evidence gets JSON. Read the <a href="/docs/methodology/">methodology</a> before quoting any number.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="agents">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Where it plugs in</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Nobody has to adopt a format. <span class="soft">Put it between the data and the model.</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">
          LDF is plain text in a prompt: no SDK, no model support needed. <code>ldf-mcp</code> puts it
          where data enters the context, and the tools and scripts on the other side keep seeing JSON.
          Only the model's copy changes, and only when it comes out shorter.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="agent-samples" data-reveal>
        <div class="sample">
          <div class="sample-bar"><span>Any MCP client</span><span class="dim">wrap a server, or serve ClickHouse and data files</span></div>
          <pre><code>{mcp}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="sample">
          <div class="sample-bar"><span>Any pipeline or model API</span><span class="dim">json, jsonl, csv, tsv, parquet in; LDF out</span></div>
          <pre><code>{install}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="sample">
          <div class="sample-bar"><span>Rust</span><span class="dim">the ldf crate</span></div>
          <pre><code>{lib}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="sample">
          <div class="sample-bar"><span>Claude Code shell output</span><span class="dim">optional hook, reversible</span></div>
          <pre><code>{hook}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="mcp-points" data-reveal>
        <div><strong>Per model.</strong> Give it the model id and it checks the measured evidence. No evidence, no conversion.</div>
        <div><strong>Primer once.</strong> The model gets a short description of the format the first time, then the data.</div>
        <div><strong>Only what helps.</strong> Small results, errors, partial output and anything not a complete dataset pass through untouched.</div>
        <div><strong>Tested clients.</strong> Claude Code, Codex CLI and OpenCode, with what each one does and does not pass through <a href="/docs/agents/">in the guide</a>.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="status">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Status</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Spec v0.2, ldf-mcp 0.5. <span class="soft">Evidence attached.</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">
          The format has been v0.2 since hoisted arrays; the releases after it changed the tools, not the
          format. One Rust workspace, published on crates.io: the codec, the per-model selector, <code>ldf-mcp</code>,
          ten baseline formats implemented faithfully (TOON passes its official
          conformance suite), and the benchmark and evaluation harnesses. Every dependency pinned.
        </p>
        <div class="cta">
          <a class="btn btn-light" href="/docs/format/">The format in five minutes <span class="arrow">→</span></a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/docs/roadmap/">What comes next</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Landing>

<style>
